    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new step and class fit the codebase's conventions cleanly:\n\n- **Right unit / additive.** A welcome *notification* (NOTIFY logger) is a distinct concern from the existing `AuditOwnerCreated` (AUDIT logger). Even though both emit `id` + `memberId`, folding the welcome emission into `AuditOwnerCreated` would turn that class into a catch-all for unrelated logging responsibilities. A dedicated `EnqueueWelcomeNotification` step is the idiomatic \"one small function class per step\" choice here, and it does not duplicate real logic \u2014 the overlap is just the field accessors.\n- **Idiomatic method/shape.** The single public `service(@Val Owner owner)` method matches the surrounding classes (`AuditOwnerCreated`, `RecordIdempotencyKey`, `AssignOwnerMemberId` all use `service` + `@Val Owner`), and the dedicated named-logger pattern mirrors `AuditOwnerCreated`'s `LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\")`.\n- **Governance consistent with the file.** Every step in `owners.POST.yml` carries `govern: [ transaction ]` (including `audit` and even `respond`), so applying it to `enqueueWelcome` follows the file's uniform convention rather than fighting it.\n- **Placement coherent.** Wired after `audit` (post-`save`, post-`assignMemberId`), so both `id` and `memberId` are populated, and the YAML comment accurately states the spec and the ordering rationale.\n\nA maintainer of this pipeline would find this a natural addition.",
